    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Polymer-modified emulsion with filler that bridges hairline cracks and fills surface texture",
      "Water cleanup before it cures and low odor compared with coal tar or oil-based products",
      "Widely stocked, so matching pails for a second coat is easy",
      "Dries to a deep, even black that hides patched areas well",
      "Label guidance on temperature and cure time is clear and conservative"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more per pail than entry-level emulsions",
      "Heavy filler content means real stirring effort, ideally with a drill paddle",
      "Coverage drops sharply on very porous, oxidized asphalt"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The safest single choice for a typical residential driveway: thick enough to do real work, easy enough for a first-timer, and sold almost everywhere.",
    "description": "Latex-ite is one of the oldest names in homeowner driveway care, and Airport Grade sits at the top of its retail range. It is a water-based asphalt emulsion with added polymer and mineral filler, which gives it more body than a basic sealer. That extra body matters on a driveway that has gone gray and slightly rough, because the filler settles into the open texture and leaves a more uniform surface instead of soaking in and disappearing. The label calls for temperatures of 50 degrees Fahrenheit and rising with dry weather ahead, which is standard for the category. Owners commonly report good color retention through the first year and say the finish is less prone to tire scuffing than cheaper pails once fully cured. The tradeoffs are price and effort: the filler settles hard in the pail and must be mixed thoroughly, and on a thirsty driveway you will use more product than the coverage figure suggests. For most people sealing a driveway themselves, it is the pail to start with."
  },
