    "bottomLine": "The right buy when the driveway is in decent shape and the goal is a clean, protective refresh at the lowest cost.",
    "description": "Henry has made asphalt-based roof and paving coatings for decades, and its driveway filler-sealer is the budget workhorse of this list. It is a water-based emulsion with enough filler to handle hairline cracks and light surface wear, and it spreads easily with a squeegee. Owners commonly describe it as a good maintenance coat for driveways that were sealed within the last few years and just need a refresh, and as less satisfying on badly oxidized surfaces, where it soaks in and needs a second coat to look even. That second coat is the real cost question. Two coats of a budget pail can end up costing about the same as one and a half coats of a premium one, so price the whole job rather than the pail. The film is thinner than flagship tiers, and you should plan on recoating on the shorter end of the usual two-to-three-year cycle. For a sound driveway and a tight budget, it does the job."
