    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most asphalt driveway sealers on a hardware store pallet are the same basic recipe: refined asphalt suspended in water with clay, filler and a little polymer. The differences that matter are how much solid material is in the pail, whether it carries sand or filler for rough surfaces, and how forgiving it is about weather. Pick on those three points and almost any reputable brand will serve you well. Pick on the number of years printed on the label and you will overpay.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Asphalt Driveway Sealers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These rankings come from manufacturer data sheets and pail labels (formula type, coverage rate, cure times and temperature limits), from whether each product is actually on shelves at the big-box stores and online right now, and from patterns in owner feedback about how the coats hold up through a winter. We did not run our own test plots, so you will not see invented durability numbers here. Where a label claim is marketing rather than a spec, we say so.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-sealer-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Sealer Can and Cannot Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Asphalt pavement is stone held together by a sticky petroleum binder. Sunlight and oxygen slowly harden that binder, which is why a new black driveway fades to gray within a few years. Hardened binder turns brittle, lets fine material wash out from between the stones, and opens small paths for water. A sealer puts a fresh, sacrificial layer of binder-like material on top. It blocks UV, sheds water, resists gasoline and oil drips for a while, and restores the color.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a sealer cannot do is repair structure. If the driveway has alligator cracking (a pattern of interconnected cracks that looks like reptile skin), sunken areas, potholes or edges crumbling into the lawn, the problem is in the base below the asphalt. Sealing over that makes it look better for a season and changes nothing about the failure underneath. Those areas need patching, partial replacement or an overlay, and a sealer comes afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest way to think about sealing is as maintenance on a sound surface. A driveway that is a few years old, mostly intact and starting to fade gets the most out of it. A driveway that is thirty years old and breaking up gets the least. If you are not sure which camp yours falls into, our breakdown of <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the real pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> walks through the cases where it pays and where it is wasted money.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pour a cup of water on the driveway on a dry day.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If it beads or runs off, the surface is still reasonably tight and can wait. If it darkens the asphalt and soaks in within a minute or two, the binder has oxidized and the surface is ready for a coat.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Timing matters too. Fresh asphalt needs time for its oils to cure out before a sealer will bond well, and most paving contractors recommend waiting somewhere between six months and a year. Our guide on <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> covers the signs that a new surface is ready.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Four Sealer Families on Store Shelves
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every retail driveway sealer belongs to one of four families. The family tells you more about how a product will behave than the brand name or the years on the label, so it is worth knowing which one you are holding before you compare prices.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Base</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch Out For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Asphalt, clay, water, emulsifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most residential driveways</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs warm, dry weather to cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polymer-modified emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Emulsion plus latex or rubber polymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher traffic, better scuff resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher price per pail</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coal tar emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Refined coal tar pitch, water, clay</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fuel and oil resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned in some states and cities; strong odor and skin irritation</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based / cutback</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Asphalt dissolved in petroleum solvent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating older, very dry asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent fumes, VOC limits, long cure</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt emulsions</strong> dominate the retail market for good reasons. They are water-based, clean up with a hose before they cure, have little odor and contain the same type of binder as the driveway itself. The cheapest versions are thin and fade fastest. The better ones add mineral filler for body and polymer for flexibility, which is what separates a premium pail from a budget pail.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coal tar sealers</strong> resist gasoline and oil better than asphalt-based products, which is why they were long the commercial standard. They also contain high levels of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, and U.S. Geological Survey research has tied coal tar sealcoat to elevated levels of those compounds in household dust and nearby streams. Washington, Minnesota and a growing list of cities and counties restrict or ban them. For a home driveway, we do not think the fuel resistance is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Oil-based sealers</strong> are less common now, partly because solvent content runs into volatile organic compound limits in some states. They soak into very dry asphalt rather than sitting on top, which some owners like on old surfaces, but they smell strongly and take longer to cure. If you are weighing the two main chemistries, our comparison of <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based versus water-based driveway sealer</a> covers the tradeoffs in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Reading a Pail Label: Years, Solids and Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest number on the front of most pails is a count of years: three, six, eight, ten. That number is best read as a product tier inside the brand, not a promise that the coat will last that long. A sealer that sees daily car traffic, a snowplow and full afternoon sun will wear faster than one on a shaded driveway that carries one car on weekends. Paving trade groups generally suggest resealing residential driveways every two to three years regardless of what the pail says.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more useful specs are on the back or on the product data sheet. Look for the coverage rate per pail, the minimum application temperature, the time before foot and vehicle traffic, and whether the product contains filler or sand. A filled sealer says so, often with words like filler-sealer, crack filling or for rough surfaces. A thin maintenance sealer typically lists higher coverage per pail because there is less solid material to spread.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage figures deserve skepticism in one direction only. They are measured on reasonably smooth, sound asphalt. An oxidized, porous surface can absorb a quarter to a third more than the label suggests on the first coat. It is very rare for a driveway to use less than the label figure, so always round your estimate up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the warranty actually covers</h3>

          <p>
            Where a sealer carries a written guarantee, it usually covers product defects and is limited to a refund or replacement of the product, not your labor or a contractor bill. Most also require that you followed the application instructions, including temperature and cure time. Keep the receipt and a photo of the batch code if the guarantee matters to you, but do not buy a pail for the warranty alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more label detail is easy to miss: the cleanup instructions. A pail that says to clean tools with water is an emulsion. One that calls for mineral spirits is solvent-based, and one that warns about skin contact, sun sensitivity or local restrictions may contain coal tar. If you cannot tell which chemistry you are holding from the label, put it back and choose a product that states it plainly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Pails You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by measuring the driveway in feet and multiplying length by width for each rectangle, then add the sections together. A typical two-car driveway runs somewhere between 400 and 800 square feet. A long rural driveway can easily pass 2,000 square feet, at which point a professional sealcoat often becomes cheaper than a stack of retail pails.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Numbers for Planning</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">250-400</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per pail, typical label range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin coats beat one heavy coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common minimum temperature, and rising</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical wait before driving</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Divide your area by the label coverage, multiply by two for two coats, and add one extra pail as a buffer for a porous surface. For a 600-square-foot driveway and a product rated at 300 square feet per pail, that works out to two pails per coat, four for two coats, and a fifth to be safe. Unopened pails keep for the next season if stored above freezing, so a spare is not wasted money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy every pail for the job at once, and if possible check that they share a batch code. Color can vary slightly between batches, and running out halfway through the second coat is the most common way a DIY job ends up looking patchy. If you are also budgeting for crack filler, a squeegee and a cleaner, our <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing cost guide</a> lays out typical totals for DIY and hired jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that frozen sealer is ruined. If the pails spent a winter in an unheated garage that dropped below freezing, the emulsion may have broken, which shows up as clumps that will not remix. Do not use a pail that will not come back to a smooth consistency after thorough stirring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Prep That Decides Whether It Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer bonds to clean asphalt. It does not bond well to dust, pollen, moss, oil or loose grit, and most premature peeling traces back to a surface that was not clean. Plan on prep taking as long as the application itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by trimming grass back from the edges with an edger or string trimmer so the sealer reaches the actual edge of the pavement. Then sweep thoroughly with a stiff push broom, blow out the debris, and wash the surface with a hose or pressure washer. A pressure washer helps on mossy or heavily soiled driveways, but keep the tip moving and back off on soft, raveling asphalt so you do not strip loose stones. If moss has taken hold, deal with it first using the steps in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-moss-from-asphalt-driveway/">removing moss from an asphalt driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil and transmission fluid stains need special treatment, because sealer will not bond over them and the oil can bleed through the coat. Scrub them with a driveway degreaser, rinse, and then apply an oil spot primer, which most sealer brands sell in small containers. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers degreasers that work on asphalt without softening it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill cracks next. Hairline cracks will be handled by a filled sealer, but anything wider than roughly an eighth of an inch should get a dedicated crack filler and time to cure before sealing. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> sorts products by crack width. Finally, let everything dry. Most labels want the surface fully dry, which after washing usually means at least a day of sun.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying It: Squeegee, Brush and Two Coats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stir each pail thoroughly before you start. Filler and sand settle to the bottom during storage, and pouring off the liquid on top leaves you with thin sealer for the first half of the job and sludge for the second. A mixing paddle on a corded drill makes this a two-minute job instead of a ten-minute arm workout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut in the edges first with a brush, working along the garage apron, the street edge and anywhere the driveway meets walkways or landscaping. Protect concrete, siding and garage doors with painter's tape and plastic sheeting, because asphalt sealer stains concrete permanently. Then pour a ribbon of sealer across the driveway and spread it with a squeegee in overlapping passes, working from the garage toward the street so you never have to step on wet sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            A rubber squeegee is the faster tool for filled sealers and leaves a smooth, even finish. A brush with stiff bristles works sealer into rough texture better and is the better choice on oxidized, porous asphalt. Many people use both: brush the first coat to push material into the surface and squeegee the second for a clean finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two thin coats outlast one thick coat. A heavy single coat skins over on top while staying soft underneath, takes far longer to cure and is more likely to crack and peel as it shrinks. Let the first coat dry to the touch, usually a few hours on a warm, sunny day, then apply the second at right angles to the first. For a step-by-step walkthrough with a tool list, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Weather, Temperature and Timing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers cure by evaporation, so weather controls the whole job. Most labels call for air and surface temperatures of at least 50 degrees Fahrenheit and rising, staying above that overnight, and a dry forecast covering a full day or more after application. Some premium products ask for 55 degrees. Read the specific pail, because the rules differ slightly by brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Late spring through early fall is the window in most of the country. Very hot days can cause problems too: when the pavement is scorching, sealer can flash-dry on top before it bonds. On those days, lightly mist the surface with a hose before applying, or start early in the morning. Our guides on <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a> and <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">the best time of year to seal</a> go deeper into regional timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain is the most common way a fresh coat gets ruined. A shower within the first few hours can wash uncured sealer into the street and leave a streaky, thin surface. If a storm catches you, our page on <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a> explains how to judge the damage and when to recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan on staying off the surface for at least a day. Foot traffic is usually fine after several hours, but most labels ask for 24 to 48 hours before cars, and longer in cool or humid weather. Turning the steering wheel while the car is stationary is what scuffs fresh sealer, so even after the wait, roll forward a little before turning for the first week. See <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a> for typical cure times by product type.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. DIY Pails vs Hiring a Sealcoater
          </h2>

          <p>
            Professional sealcoaters do not use the pails on store shelves. They buy bulk concentrate, dilute it, mix in sand and additives, and spray or squeegee it on with a tank rig. The material is often comparable to or better than premium retail pails, and a crew can finish a typical driveway in an hour or two.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY (two coats, materials only)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Pro Quote</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $100-$200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often a minimum charge, roughly $200-$350</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">800 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $200-$400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $250-$500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $350-$700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $350-$750</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $700-$1,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $600-$1,200</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices vary by region, product and how much crack repair is included, so treat those ranges as rough figures at the time of writing. The pattern is consistent, though. On small and medium driveways, DIY saves money if you count your time as free. On large driveways, the bulk material pricing of a contractor often closes the gap or beats retail entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hire, ask what product they use and whether it is coal tar or asphalt-based, how many coats they apply, whether crack filling is included, and how they handle oil spots. Be wary of door-to-door crews offering leftover material from a nearby job at a discount. That pitch is a classic driveway scam, and the product is often thinned to the point of uselessness. Our explainer on <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">professional asphalt sealcoating</a> covers what a good job looks like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Matching a Pick to Your Driveway
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Driveway in good shape, slightly faded:</strong> Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 if appearance matters most, Latex-ite Airport Grade if you want the best balance of durability and price. Either gives you a deep, even finish without a lot of filler work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gray, rough and a bit porous:</strong> a filled product like Jetcoat Premium or Latex-ite Airport Grade, brushed in for the first coat. Expect to use more product than the label suggests, and fill cracks with Pli-Stix or a pourable filler first. If your driveway is badly oxidized, our guide to <a href="/blacktop-driveway-sealer/">sealing gray, aging blacktop</a> covers the extra steps that kind of surface needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Recently sealed and just needs a refresh:</strong> Henry or Gardner. A thin coat renews color and water resistance without building up a thick layer that will eventually crack. If you are not sure how often your driveway actually needs a coat, read our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete, not asphalt:</strong> none of these. Asphalt sealer on concrete looks wrong, bonds poorly and tracks into the house. Our guide to <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">choosing a driveway sealer by surface type</a> covers both materials, and if you want to understand the chemistry behind the labels before spending money, see <a href="/best-asphalt-sealer/">asphalt sealer chemistry, retail versus commercial</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Long rural driveway or shared lane:</strong> price a contractor before buying pails. Past roughly 1,500 square feet, a crew working from bulk material often comes in close to the cost of a stack of premium pails, finishes in a morning, and spares you two days of hauling, stirring and squeegeeing. If you still prefer to do it yourself, Henry or Jetcoat keep the material bill manageable, and splitting the job into two weekends, one half at a time, keeps each session realistic for one or two people.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a driveway that is still new</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh asphalt is still releasing oils and is softer than it will be in a year. Sealer applied too early can trap those oils, bond poorly and leave a surface that scuffs easily. Wait at least six months, and ideally a full year, unless your paving contractor gave you a specific shorter interval for their mix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing every single year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More coats is not more protection. Each layer adds thickness, and a thick buildup of sealer cracks and flakes as it ages, taking the newer coats with it. Most residential driveways need a coat every two to three years. If the surface still sheds water and looks dark, skip a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the stir</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The solids in a pail of sealer settle hard during shipping and storage. Pouring off the top without mixing gives you thin, watery sealer for half the job and thick sludge for the rest, and the driveway shows both. Mix each pail with a drill paddle until the color and texture are uniform from top to bottom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying one heavy coat to save time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick coat skins over on top while staying soft beneath, which slows the cure, invites tire marks and leads to cracking as it shrinks. Two thin coats, with drying time between, give a tougher, more even surface. It costs an extra afternoon, not extra product, because two thin coats use about the same material.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based sealers need dry, warm weather to cure. A cold night or an afternoon thunderstorm within the first 24 hours can leave the coat soft, streaky or washed into the gutter. Check the hourly forecast, not just the daily high, and wait for a stretch of dry days above the label minimum temperature before you open a pail.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which asphalt driveway sealer should most homeowners buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical driveway that has started to fade, a polymer-modified, filled asphalt emulsion such as Latex-ite Airport Grade is the safest choice. It has enough body to cover moderate wear, cleans up with water, and is widely stocked so you can buy matching pails. If the driveway is smooth and you care most about the finish, Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 is the premium alternative.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is coal tar sealer better than asphalt emulsion?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Coal tar resists gasoline and oil better, which matters on commercial lots with heavy drips. For a home driveway that advantage is small, and coal tar carries real drawbacks: strong odor, skin and eye irritation during application, and high levels of compounds that research has linked to contaminated dust and runoff. Several states and many cities ban it. A good asphalt emulsion is the better home choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does an asphalt driveway sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect two to three years on most residential driveways, regardless of the number printed on the pail. Heavy traffic, full sun, snowplows and a thin first coat shorten that. Shade, light traffic and two well-applied coats lengthen it. The water test is the best guide: when water soaks in rather than beading, the surface is ready for a new coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply sealer over cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hairline cracks are fine under a filled sealer, which bridges them. Anything wider than about an eighth of an inch should be cleaned out and filled with a dedicated crack filler first, then allowed to cure. Sealer alone over an open crack simply sinks in and leaves a channel for water, and the crack will be visible again within weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pails of sealer do I need for a two-car driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure the area, divide by the coverage on the label, and double it for two coats. A 600-square-foot driveway with a product rated at 300 square feet per pail needs about four pails, plus one spare for a porous surface. Old, gray asphalt can absorb noticeably more than the label figure on the first coat, so round up rather than down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a squeegee or a brush?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A squeegee is faster and leaves a smoother finish, which suits filled sealers on a reasonably smooth driveway. A stiff brush works sealer into rough, open texture better. On older asphalt, many people brush the first coat to push material into the surface and squeegee the second coat for an even appearance. Use a brush for edges either way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can I drive on a newly sealed driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most labels call for 24 to 48 hours before vehicle traffic, and longer in cool or humid weather. Walking on it is usually fine after a few hours once it is dry to the touch. For the first week or so, avoid turning the steering wheel while the car is stopped, because that twists the tires against the soft surface and leaves scuff marks.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

